    <h2 id="connect">Connecting an app</h2>
    <p>
        The node does the radio; the app is its keyboard and screen. The phone apps and the browser app reach it
        over Bluetooth, and the browser app over USB too. The node takes one Bluetooth client and one USB client
        at a time, and both see the same contacts and messages.
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>Turn the node on, and open the app. It looks for nodes nearby: each shows as <strong>Tern</strong>.</li>
        <li>
            Choose yours. The node's screen shows a six-digit <strong>passkey</strong>: type it into the app. The
            pairing is remembered, so this is once per phone. A passkey can also be fixed in the app's node
            settings, for a board without a screen.
        </li>
        <li>
            A node new to the app asks three things: its <strong>region</strong>, which it must have; whether it is a{" "}
            <strong>leaf</strong> or a <strong>relay</strong>; and, if you like, a first contact.
        </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        The phone apps keep the link up in the background and notify you of new messages, and you can reply from
        the notification. The browser app works only while its page is open. The node keeps your recent messages
        itself, so an app that was away catches up when it connects again.
    </p>

    <h2 id="settings">Region, role and power</h2>
    <dl>
        <dt>Region</dt>
        <dd>
            The frequency and the rules the node sends by: US915 (the United States and Canada), EU868 (Europe),
            AU915 (Australia) or NZ915 (New Zealand). It must be the one where the node is used, and nodes only hear
            others in the same region. In EU868 a node may send for at most 10% of any hour, and counts its time on
            the air to keep to it.
        </dd>
        <dt>Role</dt>
        <dd>
            A <strong>leaf</strong> sends and receives its own messages only: best for a node you carry. A{" "}
            <strong>relay</strong> also passes on messages for others, which lets the mesh reach further but uses
            more battery: best for a node left somewhere high, on power. You can change it at any time.
        </dd>
        <dt>Transmit power</dt>
        <dd>
            How loud the node sends. It starts low, enough for a desk; raise it for distance. The node refuses a
            power that, with the antenna's gain, is more than the region allows.
        </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>Each is changed from the app's node screen.</p>

    <h2 id="contacts">Contacts</h2>
    <p>
        A node's address is its public key: sixty-four hex digits, which nobody types. People exchange them instead
        as a <strong>link</strong> (<code>ternmesh.org/a/…</code>) or its <strong>QR code</strong>, which the node's{" "}
        <em>Share</em> screen and every app show. To add someone, scan their code, open their link, or paste it into
        the app's add form, and give them a name. The name is yours: it never goes on the air.
    </p>
    <p>
        Beside every address is its <strong>short code</strong>, twelve digits. Compare it with the person, face to
        face or on a call: if it matches, the address you hold is really theirs. It is a quick check; the full
        address in the QR code is the strong one.
    </p>
    <p>
        Your node takes a first message from anyone you have saved as a contact. Someone you have not saved may be
        turned away once your node has conversations going; the app tells you who asked, so you can add them and
        try again.
    </p>

    <h2 id="messages">Messages</h2>
    <p>
        A message holds up to 128 bytes of text: about 128 letters of English, fewer with accents or emoji. Each one
        you send shows where it is:
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>State</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Waiting</td>
                    <td>
                        Not on the air yet, and the app says why: looking for a route to them, setting up a secure
                        session, the region's transmit limit, or the radio busy. It goes by itself once it can.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Sent</td>
                    <td>A node on the way was heard passing it on. It has left your node, and not yet arrived.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Delivered</td>
                    <td>Their node says it has it: the acknowledgement came back all the way.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Not delivered</td>
                    <td>
                        Your node tried for a while and gave up. It may be out of reach, off, or unable to set up a
                        session: see <a href="/docs/troubleshooting#not-delivered">Troubleshooting</a>.
                    </td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        A message is delivered to their node, which keeps it until their app collects it, so they need not be
        connected when you write.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sessions">Secure sessions, and how many</h2>
    <p>
        The first time two nodes write, they set up a <strong>secure session</strong>: four short frames, two or three
        seconds, after which only those two nodes can read what passes between them. It happens by itself, and the
        message waits for it.
    </p>
    <p>
        A node holds a session with each person it writes to, and has room for a fixed number: 8 on the current
        release. Once it is full, writing to someone new is <em>not delivered</em> until you end a session you no
        longer need, from that conversation in the app (<strong>End session</strong>). Ending one costs nothing but a
        new handshake the next time you write. The next release raises this to 16 sessions on ESP32-S3 and nRF52840
        boards, with 32 contacts and 8 groups.
    </p>

    <h2 id="groups">Groups</h2>
    <p>
        A group is a conversation among everyone who holds its secret. Make one in the app and invite contacts to
        it, over their sessions; or show its <strong>join code</strong>, a link and a QR code, and let people join
        from that.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
        Anyone who sees a join code can read the group, now and later. Show it only to people you mean to let in,
        and make a new group if it leaks.
    </p>
    <p>
        In a group, each message carries who wrote it as that node claims, and the app shows a contact's name where
        it knows the writer. Leaving a group is quiet: the others are not told. Tern has no public channel that
        everyone is in; to find people nearby, see the next section.
    </p>

    <h2 id="about">Who is about</h2>
    <p>
        A node can send a <strong>presence card</strong> now and then: its address and a name, so that people near it
        can find it and add it. The app lists the cards your node has heard, each name shown as its sender's claim
        beside the address's short code.
    </p>
    <p>
        Your own card is off until you turn it on. On, it puts your node's address and the name you give it on the
        air in clear, every couple of hours, for anyone listening. Turn it off again from the same switch.
    </p>

    <h2 id="positions">Sharing your position</h2>
    <p>
        You choose who sees where you are, and how exactly: share with a contact or a group, at a precision from a
        few metres to a whole region, for a time or until you stop. The phone gives the node its location only while
        the node shares with someone, and the node rounds it to each person's precision before anything goes on the
        air. Positions shared with you appear on the app's map.
    </p>
    <p>
        The map's tiles come from <a href="https://openfreemap.org">OpenFreeMap</a> on Android and from Apple on
        iPhone, so those servers see which area the map shows. The <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a> has the
        rest.
    </p>

    <h2 id="node">The node's screen and button</h2>
    <p>
        The node works on its own too. Its screen has a few pages, and one button, marked <strong>PRG</strong> on a
        Heltec V3:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Press</strong> it to show the next page. A press while the screen is dark only wakes it.</li>
        <li><strong>Hold</strong> it for a second to act on the page shown: the next message, the next nodes, a
            group's join code.</li>
        <li><strong>Hold</strong> it for five seconds to turn the node off; the screen counts down from the second,
            and letting go keeps it on. A press turns it on again.</li>
    </ul>
    <dl>
        <dt>Home</dt>
        <dd>New messages, how many nodes it hears, the battery and the region.</dd>
        <dt>Messages</dt>
        <dd>One at a time, newest first, with what became of those you sent.</dd>
        <dt>Nearby</dt>
        <dd>The nodes it hears directly, and how well.</dd>
        <dt>Air</dt>
        <dd>How much of the region's limit on the air is used.</dd>
        <dt>Share</dt>
        <dd>Its address as a QR code, and its short code: what someone scans to add you.</dd>
        <dt>This node</dt>
        <dd>Its short code and address, role, region, power and firmware version.</dd>
        <dt>Groups</dt>
        <dd>The groups it holds; hold PRG to show one's join code.</dd>
        <dt>Phones</dt>
        <dd>The phones paired with it; hold PRG twice to forget them all.</dd>
        <dt>Reset</dt>
        <dd>
            Erases the node for a new owner: hold PRG, then hold it again within ten seconds. It starts again with a
            new address, and everything it held is gone.
        </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
        The LED blinks for every frame sent or heard, and briefly every four seconds while a message is unread. At
        10% battery Home says <strong>Low</strong>, and below 3.3 V the node turns itself off rather than run the cell
        flat. Boards without a screen or a button, listed on <a href="/docs/hardware">Hardware</a>, are used from an
        app alone.
    </p>

    <h2 id="updates">Updating the firmware</h2>
    <p>
        When a newer release is out, the apps offer it on the node screen and send it over Bluetooth: a few minutes,
        with the node working as usual meanwhile. If the link drops, it goes on from where it stopped. The node keeps
        its address, contacts, sessions, groups, messages and settings, and goes back to the old firmware by itself if
        the new one will not start.
    </p>
    <p>
        Updating over Bluetooth works on ESP32 boards that were flashed with release 0.2.0 or later. An older one is
        updated once from <a href="/flash">the flash page</a>, choosing <em>Update</em>, which keeps its address; nRF52840
        boards are updated by copying the release's <code>.uf2</code> file onto them, as when they were first flashed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="console">The serial console</h2>
    <p>
        For the curious: a node plugged in over USB has a text console at 115200 baud, where <code>status</code>,{" "}
        <code>routes</code>, <code>peers</code> and a dozen more commands show and do what the apps do, and some they
        do not. The list is in the <a href={`${fw}/tree/main/ports/esp32#using-it`}>firmware's README</a>.
    </p>
